WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.260
Once, back when we were little,&nbsp;
we all learned that Asia is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.360
largest and most populated continent in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.740
and that within Asia was also the most&nbsp;
populous country on the planet: China.
 &nbsp;

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.780
However, this is no longer the case.
 
(India overtakes China to become the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.320
most populous country – The Guardian)
 
Indeed, what was long predicted has finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:28.320
happened: India has overtaken China in population.
According to estimates, in July 2023, India&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.420
already outnumbers China by a mere 3 million&nbsp;
people but this gap will increase over time.
 &nbsp;

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.680
To give you an idea, experts estimate that&nbsp;
China's population will fall steadily to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:44.040
800 million by the end of the century. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
India will reach 1.7 billion by 2060 and will end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:49.080
the century with about 1.5 billion inhabitants.
 
In other words, by the end of the 21st century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.140
India's population will be&nbsp;
practically double that of China.
 &nbsp;

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.700
So what does this mean? For China, it&nbsp;
is a challenge. For example, by 2050,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:01.620
there will be 700 million workers in China,&nbsp;
200 million fewer than in 2011, and these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:07.380
700 million will also have to face the costs of&nbsp;
having 500 million Chinese over the age of 60,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:12.300
that’s 300 million more than there are today.&nbsp;
And, not only that, China also risks losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:16.740
one of its greatest strengths: cheap labor.
 
Even so, we are not going to talk to you about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.600
China: today we are going to talk to you about the&nbsp;
new demographic champion that India has become.
 &nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.480
A country that is still very poor and still&nbsp;
has a long way to go to match China in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.100
economic productivity or standard of living.
 
And, the question is, why is that? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.580
we could highlight many factors:
 
Excessive bureaucracy, aggressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.840
protectionism, the caste system we recently&nbsp;
told you about, and also its chronic inability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.460
to develop one crucial factor for economic&nbsp;
development: having robust infrastructure.
 &nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.920
Which is precisely what we are&nbsp;
going to dedicate this video to.
 &nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.080
But VisualPolitik viewers, how did India get here?&nbsp;
How is Narendra Modi dealing with the challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.300
of a chronic lack of infrastructure? Could India&nbsp;
become the next global manufacturing epicenter?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.260
Which industry could put Xi Jinping's&nbsp;
geopolitical plans in check? Today we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.620
will answer all these questions, but&nbsp;
first let's look at a bit of history.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.340
And by the way, if you want to discover&nbsp;
a unique kind of content here on YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.700
literally changing the world for the&nbsp;
better, stay watching until the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.480
of this video. There, we have a special&nbsp;
recommendation from our friends at Planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:26.400
Wild that you shouldn't miss.
 
 
(THE INDIAN POLITICAL DYNASTY)
 &nbsp;

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.280
Let's start with a question&nbsp;
that may catch you off guard:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.380
what do you know about the history of India?
 
Surely many of you know some facts about Mahatma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.700
Gandhi and his commitment to nonviolent protest.&nbsp;
And, I'm sure most of you also know that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.260
country was a former British colony. But...&nbsp;
Anything else? Most probably not much more.
 &nbsp;

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.760
Typically India is only highlighted for&nbsp;
its huge population, yet this giant is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.840
much more than that and its history is&nbsp;
crucial for understanding today's India.
 &nbsp;

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:02.880
Well, given that its independence from the&nbsp;
British Empire happened in 1947 under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.260
leadership of Gandhi, India is a relatively&nbsp;
young federal state. From the very beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:12.120
it had to face a very unproductive economic&nbsp;
scenario based on subsistence agriculture;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.440
up to 70% of the population lived&nbsp;
on subsistence farming. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:21.420
after Gandhi's assassination in 1948, his&nbsp;
companion and leading independence figure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:26.640
Jawarharlal Nehru, came to power and India&nbsp;
began its path to economic development.&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.080
India's new prime minister began to run the&nbsp;
economy from within the State and in 1951&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.720
created the country's first five-year plan. This&nbsp;
plan nationalized mining, the energy sector and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.280
heavy industry. All of these were classified&nbsp;
as strategic sectors. A strict licensing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.140
system was also implemented, which increased the&nbsp;
bureaucracy to an excessive degree. All this meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:51.480
that during his term of office, which lasted&nbsp;
until 1964, the economy barely managed to grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:57.300
by 2.5%. This was insufficient to put an end to&nbsp;
chronic unemployment, poverty and malnutrition.
 &nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:07.320
Later, with the death of Nehru in 1964 and that&nbsp;
of his successor, Lal Bahadur Shastri, in 1966,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:12.180
came another of the most important figures&nbsp;
in the country's history: Indira Gandhi. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:15.240
she is not the daughter of the&nbsp;
Mahatma, but of Nehru himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:26.400
Indira Gandhi ruled from 1966 to 1984 almost&nbsp;
uninterrupted, except for the three years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:32.340
between 1977 and 1980. During the rest of the&nbsp;
time she was lady and master of the country.
 &nbsp;

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.940
So what did she do during her two&nbsp;
terms in office? Well, basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:41.100
she continued with her father's economic plans:&nbsp;
she nationalized the banking system, the coal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.660
mines and the oil companies, among others.
 
Even so, in spite of all this, surely the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:50.580
main milestone of the Indian prime minister was&nbsp;
the eradication, almost completely, of hunger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:54.540
through the well-known Green Revolution.
 
And what did this revolution consist of?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:58.980
Essentially the introduction of crops with&nbsp;
much higher yields, such as dwarf rice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:04.080
the widespread use of fertilizers and pesticides,&nbsp;
and the improvement of irrigation systems.&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:10.200
However, in October 1984, four months after a&nbsp;
clash between the state and Sikh separatists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.800
she was killed by some of her bodyguards&nbsp;
who professed the same religion.
 &nbsp;

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.300
But, VisualPolitik viewers, if you thought that&nbsp;
political dynasties were only a Kennedy thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:22.140
you're wrong. Do you know who took the&nbsp;
reins of the country after Indira Gandhi's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:27.600
assassination? That's right, her son, Rajiv&nbsp;
Gandhi. And what exactly did he do? He didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.800
really change too many things, he continued&nbsp;
the legacy of his mother and grandfather.&nbsp;

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:39.960
It turned out, however, that India managed to&nbsp;
grow above 3% during the 1980s. And it even had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.340
some reasonably good years. The development given&nbsp;
the country's poverty level was nothing special,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:48.240
but apparently not a disaster either.
 
Nevertheless the problem was that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.380
growth had a catch to it. For years,&nbsp;
India grew thanks to debt. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:58.380
as happened in Latin America in the early 1980s,&nbsp;
India suffered a financial crisis in 1990.
 &nbsp;

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:03.420
It was then that, in 1991, under the&nbsp;
leadership of Prime Minister Narasimha Rao,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:08.820
India began to open up to the world: bureaucracy&nbsp;
was drastically reduced by eliminating much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:13.560
Nehru's licensing system, tariffs were lowered,&nbsp;
and currency reserves eventually grew from $3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:20.940
billion in 1991 to $25 billion in 1995.
 
Even so, India still lacked something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:25.740
would consolidate and expand its economic growth,&nbsp;
something that neighboring China was already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:31.800
developing: infrastructure. Well, thus began the&nbsp;
infrastructure drive in this Asian subcontinent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:39.360
Pay close attention:
 
("MAKE IN INDIA")
 &nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:47.520
A country needs infrastructure: without&nbsp;
roads, power plants or technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:52.200
it is difficult for a country to prosper in the&nbsp;
21st century. That's why countries in Africa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.980
are working so hard with China, because&nbsp;
the Asian giant is providing them with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.980 --> 00:07:00.540
loans and assistance for building all the basic&nbsp;
infrastructure that is essential for development.
 &nbsp;

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:05.820
In India, Prime Minister Atal Bihari&nbsp;
Vajpayee became aware of all this in 1998,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:09.060
and therefore launched the famous&nbsp;
"Golden Quadrilateral" project.&nbsp;

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:14.280
It’s a project that connects by road four of the&nbsp;
country's main cities: New Delhi in the north,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.900
Kolkata in the east, Chennai in the south&nbsp;
and Bombay in the west, all with a view to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:23.700
improving economic exchanges within the country.
 
But, of course, this momentum did not stop there.
 &nbsp;

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:28.140
His successor, Manmohan Singh, who came to&nbsp;
power in 2004, promoted the construction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:33.540
of corridors dedicated to rail freight.
 
In fact, it made these the main focus of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:40.080
the 2007-2012 five-year plan, and today two&nbsp;
of these corridors are already in operation:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:46.740
the western corridor, which is 1,500 km long,&nbsp;
that’s 932 miles, and the eastern corridor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:53.280
which is the longest. The 1,900 km or 1,180&nbsp;
mile long corridor runs from Ludhiana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:58.380
the most populous city in Punjab, to Dankuni,&nbsp;
which is part of the Kolkata metropolitan area.
 &nbsp;

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:02.160
However, in spite of all this, the truth&nbsp;
is that progress was still too slow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:10.260
And of course, in such a densely populated&nbsp;
country with extraordinarily large cities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:15.000
you can imagine what a huge problem that is:&nbsp;
congested roads, crowded trains running at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.220
extremely slow speeds, power cuts, problems with&nbsp;
delivery times of goods, and so on and so forth.
 &nbsp;

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:24.900
Well, it was then, suddenly, in 2014 when&nbsp;
one particular figure who we have already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:29.940
told you about on this channel burst into&nbsp;
Indian national politics: Narendra Modi.
 &nbsp;

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:33.240
In other videos we have already talked&nbsp;
about how Modi is managing the country:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:38.280
complaints about the violation of human rights&nbsp;
are constant and many of his economic promises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:42.480
came to practically nothing. Yet he is still&nbsp;
a very popular politician in the country.
 &nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:47.100
And one of his great efforts in particular&nbsp;
has been and continues to be to improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:51.900
infrastructure. And not like his predecessors,&nbsp;
but on a massive scale. All under one initiative:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.900 --> 00:09:00.900
the "Make in India" initiative.
 
Launched in 2014 by Modi himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:05.700
the "Make in India" initiative aims to make India&nbsp;
the most important manufacturing hub in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:10.260
even above China. The aim is to increase the&nbsp;
presence of the manufacturing sector from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:16.620
current 17% to more than 25%. Along the way, they&nbsp;
hope to create 100 million jobs in this sector.&nbsp;

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:20.460
To this end, the Indian government has&nbsp;
focused on building better infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:25.680
that is capable of attracting more investment and&nbsp;
encouraging innovation in 25 specific sectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.360
including energy, chemicals, electronics and&nbsp;
the automotive sector.For the time being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:37.320
the initiative seems to be paying off, as&nbsp;
from FY 2012-13 to 2020-21, the main year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:44.100
of the Covid pandemic, Foreign Direct Investment&nbsp;
went from about $34 billion to touch $82 billion.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:49.380
During this time, Modi has pushed&nbsp;
for the construction of more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:57.180
10,000 km or 6,200 miles of new highways every&nbsp;
year, more than 45 new civil airports have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:01.980
built and dozens more are under construction.&nbsp;
New ports, new metro transport systems and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:06.360
also thousands and thousands of kilometers of new&nbsp;
railroads have been developed across the country.
 &nbsp;

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:11.340
The aim is to also achieve one crucial objective:&nbsp;
to reduce the country's logistics costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:17.760
which currently stand at 14% of GDP, to&nbsp;
less than 8% of GDP by 2030. Efficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:21.840
infrastructure is absolutely key to the&nbsp;
competitiveness of the industrial sector.
 &nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:26.400
You see, VisualPolitik community, Modi has&nbsp;
set the wheels in motion for India to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:32.340
to overtake China not only in population, but&nbsp;
also in manufacturing. That is his big goal.
 &nbsp;

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:35.760
So for the Indian prime minister&nbsp;
the race has only just begun.&nbsp;

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:44.640
In fact, the 2023 budget dedicated&nbsp;
to infrastructure construction has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:51.780
grown by 33% over the previous year&nbsp;
to 3.3% of GDP, or $122 billion.
 &nbsp;

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:55.920
In addition, they have also decided&nbsp;
to extend interest-free loans to 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:59.460
years for all state governments that&nbsp;
want to invest in infrastructure.
 &nbsp;

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:02.580
And, not only that, investment&nbsp;
in artificial intelligence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:06.420
especially for its incorporation&nbsp;
into education, is also one of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.740
fundamental axes. Not surprisingly they will&nbsp;
continue to invest in the freight corridors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:16.560
both in the two that are almost finished and are&nbsp;
now operating at almost 100%, as well as in three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:20.640
other projects that have already been approved.
 
Yet despite all the infrastructure construction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:24.060
there is one problem that could&nbsp;
drive investment away from India:

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:30.720
(Analysis: Dark summer nights: India faces&nbsp;
high risks of power cuts after years of coal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:33.480
hydro power neglect – Reuters)
 
We are, of course, talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:40.920
electricity supply. India has been having&nbsp;
power cuts for at least three years now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:46.140
mostly at night, due to the shortage of coal and&nbsp;
the country’s poor and outdated infrastructure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:50.100
As you can imagine, this is a fundamental&nbsp;
problem for the entire industrial sector.
 &nbsp;

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:55.200
To solve this problem, India plans to build&nbsp;
26 coal-fired power plants over the next 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:59.820
years. Yes, that’s what I said, coal.
 
Be that as it may, it will most likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:03.120
take decades for India to catch up&nbsp;
with China as the world's leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:07.440
manufacturing producer, if it ever does.
 
Of course, this does not mean that India&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:11.880
will not become as crucial an economic player as&nbsp;
China is today. There is even one industry that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:16.920
if developed in India, could disrupt many of&nbsp;
Beijing's geopolitical plans. Check it out:
 &nbsp;

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:23.940
(THE GEOPOLITICS OF THE SEMICONDUCTOR)

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:28.560
[Host: Say this with a little&nbsp;
irony] Here’s a question for you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:31.080
hackers: what electronic&nbsp;
component does a cell phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:35.580
a car or even a guided missile need in order&nbsp;
to work? I’m sure some of you won’t know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.160
but it is something we have already&nbsp;
talked about on our sister channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:42.300
VisualEconomik. If you still haven’t checked&nbsp;
it out, I’ll leave the link in the description.

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:47.040
We are talking, of course, about semiconductors.&nbsp;
And do you know which country has all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:51.540
the potential to become a semiconductor&nbsp;
production hotspot? You guessed it, India.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:56.040
You see, Modi does not intend to create&nbsp;
and boost companies from scratch in India,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:59.880
but rather to attract large firms to the&nbsp;
country. And that's not crazy either.

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:04.980
Think about it, they have four advantages: a large&nbsp;
domestic market, extensive English proficiency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:09.540
a relatively cheap labor force and one of&nbsp;
the largest pools of science, technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:13.860
engineering and mathematics graduates.
For all these reasons, it is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:17.220
surprising that we are already&nbsp;
encountering news stories like these:

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:23.820
(ISMC Digital to invest $3 billion in&nbsp;
chip-making unit in Karnataka – EconomicTimes)

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:28.980
(Foxconn and Vedanta to build $19&nbsp;
billion India chip factory – BBC)

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:33.000
And it is precisely this that is&nbsp;
one of China's greatest fears.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.400
But the question is, why? Well, as you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:40.560
India and China are not exactly the world's best&nbsp;
friends: they have conflicting territorial claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:44.160
and both countries aspire to become&nbsp;
the leading regional power in Asia.

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:52.260
For example, China has been expanding&nbsp;
its power in the South China Sea and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:56.700
even more worrying for India, into other&nbsp;
Asian countries such as Pakistan and Sri&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:59.940
Lanka. But for its part, India has&nbsp;
also been doing something similar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:04.440
playing on its geostrategic position in&nbsp;
the Andaman Islands and strengthening ties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:09.240
with countries such as Oman, the Seychelles&nbsp;
and Indonesia, crucial locations that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:14.580
eventually be used to cut off the supply of crude&nbsp;
oil coming to China by sea from the Persian Gulf.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:16.560
We are talking about a budding struggle that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:19.560
inevitably brought India ever&nbsp;
closer to the United States:

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:23.160
(Biden makes trade-offs and Modi steps out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.040
his comfort zone during elaborate&nbsp;
state visit at White House – CNN)

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:29.880
But take note! This approach is&nbsp;
not only in Modi's own interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:32.940
but also in the interest of&nbsp;
the Biden administration:

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:39.000
("The United States needs India as a strategic&nbsp;
partner to balance against China in both South&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:44.580
Asia and the Indo-Pacific, and India needs the&nbsp;
United States as it develops a more prosperous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:49.380
greener future" – Caroline Grey,&nbsp;
analyst, Eurasia Group, 2023)

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:53.940
We are talking about an alliance that Washington&nbsp;
considers absolutely key for containing China.

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:58.260
Not only because of the enormous economic and&nbsp;
political influence that India will acquire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:02.580
in the coming years and decades, or because&nbsp;
this country could be a natural alternative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:06.660
to the dependence that the Western world&nbsp;
has developed towards Chinese factories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:10.980
but also for another very simple reason:&nbsp;
Taiwan's geopolitical vulnerability.

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:15.120
And, you may be wondering, what does&nbsp;
Taiwan have to do with this? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:19.020
a lot: remember that Taiwan is the&nbsp;
world's largest semiconductor producer.

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:27.120
If Taiwanese production were to come to a&nbsp;
sudden halt due to a possible Chinese invasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:31.320
the world would panic and, perhaps,&nbsp;
a major part of the economy would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:35.640
also come to a halt. Semiconductors are&nbsp;
crucial to industry around the world.

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:40.260
Precisely for this reason, the United States is&nbsp;
investing heavily in this industry. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:44.220
in August last year, it passed legislation&nbsp;
that included an investment of more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:48.180
$50 billion to strengthen its own&nbsp;
semiconductor production. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:52.740
also pushing for Taiwanese companies such as&nbsp;
TSMC to move part of their operations there.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:59.460
(TSMC to up Arizona investment to $40 billion&nbsp;
with second semiconductor chip plant – CNBC)

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:04.920
Today, the United States produces about 12% of all&nbsp;
semiconductors in the world, far behind Taiwan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:09.480
an island that also produces the vast majority&nbsp;
of the world's most advanced semiconductors.

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:17.280
Well, if, as intended, chip production&nbsp;
in countries such as the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:20.820
Japan and India, as well as&nbsp;
South Korea, were to increase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:25.620
China would lose an important strategic&nbsp;
asset. Its potential ability to threaten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:29.400
Western economies with the invasion&nbsp;
of Taiwan would be greatly reduced.

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:35.640
And not only that. If India manages to position&nbsp;
itself in this field, that would probably also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:40.320
encourage many technology companies to relocate&nbsp;
their current production from China to India.

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:44.760
And as you can see in this whole story,&nbsp;
infrastructure is absolutely crucial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:49.020
India needs to connect to the world and&nbsp;
make its economy agile and affordable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:52.440
It needs professionals and goods to&nbsp;
be able to move quickly and cheaply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:56.100
and it also needs to ensure an&nbsp;
abundant and competitive power supply.

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:58.800
And now, having come this far,&nbsp;
the questions are over to you:

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:03.660
Do you think India will also manage to overtake&nbsp;
China economically, become a major semiconductor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:07.980
producer and reduce the risk posed by China?&nbsp;
You can leave us your answers in the comments.

